Probe records use `option name` as their unique status key. `option interface`
selects a netifd logical interface, defaulting to the name for compatibility.
It may contain ordered aliases separated by whitespace, such as
`wan6 wan_6 wan` for the configured IPv6 uplink and its protocol-created child.
The first eligible alias is selected; this is not health-based failover.
Configure independent uplinks as independent records.

`option family ipv4|ipv6` selects the address family (default ipv4).
Use distinct records for each family or destination. `require_default 1`
requires an active unicast default route of that family before probing.
It cannot be combined with `ignore_ubus 1`, which selects a raw device.
Numeric probe destinations avoid DNS availability affecting status queries.

The ubus `pingcheck status` method reports ONLINE if any configured probe
succeeds, UNKNOWN while a probe or its authoritative network owner is pending,
and OFFLINE after all configured paths are known unavailable or timed out.
An empty configuration is UNKNOWN. Notifications on `pingcheck.state` signal
changes; consumers should subscribe first and query the current status.

The existing online/offline shell hooks retain INTERFACE as the probe key and
DEVICE as the resolved device. Detailed ubus status also includes
logical_interface, family and host. The LED consumer uses ubus, not shell hooks.
